WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 position:97%
Me, as an architect and the professors like Mauricio Brocato as engineers,

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:22.000 position:97%
we realized that Mathematica was a highly privileged sharing platform that can allow different people,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:32.000 position:98%
with different backgrounds, and different specific interests in their research,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000 position:77%
but that share a common vision, to come together.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.000 position:98%
I am currently working as an associate professor at the National School of Architecture at Paris-Malaquais.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.000 position:73%
I had my first contact with Mathematica in 2003

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:58.000 position:98%
during a scenography project that had been commissioned by the Pompidou center,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:11.000 position:98%
where we had to solve architectural formalism problems from a mathematical angle.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.000 position:97%
But with applications that vary based on the evolution of my work.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:32.000 position:98%
It started over right away when I was working on my thesis in architecture,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000 position:88%
which led to the French graduate degree in architecture.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000 position:98%
In the thesis, I worked on the problem of surfaces, on the problematic surfaces in contemporary architecture,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.000 position:97%
and that work was entirely written in a unique Mathematica notebook.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000 position:97%
I would say that was the continuation of the first experience.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:07.000 position:97%
And I have also tried to introduce Mathematica increasingly in my teaching

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.000 position:77%
at the School of Architecture at Paris-Malaquais.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.000 position:92%
We realized that Mathematica was a very privileged platform

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000 position:81%
because we were able handle extremely variable data.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000 position:97%
In that school I try, since the curriculum is related to urbanism,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:36.000 position:98%
I try also to encourage students to work on Mathematica to do image analysis.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:45.000 position:91%
For various reasons, the classic softwares of architecture

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:58.000 position:98%
have a whole bunch of concepts of architecture that are built-in within the software.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000 position:97%
Simple things such as columns, beams, doors, windows, insulation, thickness of the wall,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000 position:97%
and a lot of things like that that are very architecture-oriented and are very convenient.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:18.000 position:97%
And in the architectural education context, I see these things as too practical.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000 position:97%
They do not leave enough room for architectural experimentation,

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:32.000 position:98%
both more formal, more mathematized in some cases but also more conceptual.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:40.000 position:97%
With Mathematica you can explain to students how to draw a circle,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000 position:97%
which is the same as what s/he learned in high school with a Cartesian equation.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:04:00.000 position:97%
We can do it with a parametric equation, but we can also do it in assembling four pieces of Bézier curves to draw the same geometric figure.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.000 position:97%
We have access to a rich geometry, and I would say a variety of mathematical tools

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.000 position:98%
that are not accessible in regular modeling geometric software.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000 position:98%
And the third big advantage for me is the format of Mathematica

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:29.000 position:98%
the notebook, the interactivity that allows you to do everything in it.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:37.000 position:98%
You can write, you can import images, I won't say you can send sms, but almost.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.000 position:67%
You can... query databases on the internet.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.000 position:97%
Especially now with the coupling between the notebook and Wolfram|Alpha,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000 position:77%
all those things seem absolutely essential to me.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:01.000 position:97%
And open teaching, and open, in a case, give students an opening to a world of data,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:08.000 position:97%
to a world of knowledge that is completely different from what they were used to before.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:17.000 position:97%
That's also what we are trying to teach our students. To remain relatively open.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:25.000 position:98%
Mathematica seems to be quite suitable, even a very suitable platform to explore,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000 position:56%
to have a very exploratory approach.

